import{_ as t,a,o as n,M as o}from"./chunks/framework.DYNaguLt.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"The name of the rose","description":"The power of words, the nature of identity and the essence of being.","frontmatter":{"title":"The name of the rose","subtitle":"and the big ball of mud","illustration":"rose","description":"The power of words, the nature of identity and the essence of being.","topics":["domain driven design"],"date":"2025-11-23T00:00:00.000Z","head":[["meta",{"name":"description","content":"The power of words, the nature of identity and the essence of being."}],["meta",{"name":"keywords","content":"software domain driven design"}]],"wordCount":1095,"readingTime":"6 minute read"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"on/the-name-of-the-rose/index.md","filePath":"on/the-name-of-the-rose/index.md"}'),r={name:"on/the-name-of-the-rose/index.md"};function i(s,e,h,m,l,c){return n(),a("div",null,e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<p>Naming things is hard. So hard, in fact, that it is considered (by programmers) to be one of the two hardest problems in computer science. Lots of people (programmers) agree on this. Ask anyone (programmers) what they think the hardest thing in computer science is. <em>Naming things</em>, is what they&#39;ll say.</p><p>On the surface it might seem like this is true. Indeed, all but the very simplest software requires a dizzying myriad of variables, functions, classes, commands, constants, arguments, packages and namespaces to be written into existence in total lexicographic harmony.</p><p>Advice on the matter is curiously reminiscent of dialogue from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catch-22" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Catch-22</a>. <em>Identifiers</em>, they&#39;ll say, <em>should be</em>:</p><ul><li><p>really <em>descriptive</em> and <em>meaningful</em> so that software archaeologists in a hundred years&#39; time will know <em>precisely</em> what was intended, with a healthy amount of of <em>units</em> and <em>types</em> and <em>actions</em> and <em>nouns</em> and so forth;</p></li><li><p><em>concise</em> and <em>compact</em> because other programmers are always very <em>busy</em> and don&#39;t have time to actually read your code;</p></li><li><p>don&#39;t use abbreviations, thank you very much;</p></li><li><p>... except, <em>of course</em>, for <code>HTTP</code>, <code>API</code>, <code>REST</code>, <code>ASCII</code>, <code>A11Y</code>, <code>I18N</code>, <code>CRUD</code>, <code>SDK</code>, <code>XML</code>, <code>TCP</code>, <code>YAML</code>, or <code>Y2K</code>, for example — <em>never</em>, <em>ever</em> un-abbreviate those ones;</p></li><li><p>always keep your <em>camels</em> separate from your <em>kebabs</em> and your <em>snakes</em>;</p></li><li><p>above all be perfectly <em>consistent</em>, except make it totally <em>unique</em> because otherwise there will be a namespace collision.</p></li></ul><p>William Shakespeare (not a programmer) thought that naming things was <em>easy</em>.</p><blockquote><p>What’s in a name? That which we call a rose<br> By any other name would smell as sweet;<br><br><a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1513/pg1513-images.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The Tragedy of Romeo and Juliet</a></p></blockquote><p>Described as &quot;the most influential lexical originator in the history of English&quot;, Shakespeare is directly attributed with inventing almost <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/files/9916/1953/2477/Extract_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">two thousand words</a>.</p><p>Far be it from anyone to argue with <em>The Bard</em>, however, commenting on studies from Oxford University&#39;s crossmodal laboratory, prominent <a href="https://www.psy.ox.ac.uk/research/crossmodal-research-laboratory" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>gastrophysicists</em></a> note that the speech sounds that are present in the brand names of alcoholic beverages can &quot;<a href="https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2023/07/qa-professor-charles-spence-the-sensuous-drinker/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">almost subliminally set taste expectations</a>&quot;.</p><p>From Wageningen University in the Netherlands <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0950329316301707" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>What’s in a name? The effect of congruent and incongruent product names on liking and emotions when consuming beer or non-alcoholic beer in a bar</em></a> relates that the mere labeling of actual beer as non-alcoholic beer emphatically tanked participants&#39; ratings of the experience, feeling overall less <em>comforted</em> or <em>exuberant</em>, and <em>disappointed</em>.</p><p>A study from 1924, <a href="https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/210861794.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Ein experimenteller Beitrag zum Problem der psychologischen Grundlagen der Namengebung</em></a>, describes an experiment in which participants were shown a series of drawings and asked to select from a list of nonsense words the word they associated with or felt most closely resembled the image. In what has become known as the <em>takete-maluma</em> or <em>bouba-kiki</em> phenomenon, the significant overlap between participants&#39; responses implies certain regularities &quot;which the human soul follows in the process of name-giving&quot;.</p><p>Philosophically speaking, the debate over whether words have an <em>intrinsic</em> relation to the things they signify versus an arbitrary value determined by <em>convention</em> has been going back and forth for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cratylus_(dialogue)" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">several thousand years</a>. In the 13th century, William of Ockham, argued that universals (like names or categories) do not have an independent existence apart from the objects they refer to, in opposition to Platonic realism. Writing in the 20th century, Ludwig Wittgenstein explored how the meaning of a word is deeply tied to its use in language, emphasising that <em>context</em> determines the meaning of words, and that meaning is therefore contingent on social interaction, or <em>convention</em>. In <em>Phenomenology</em>, Edmund Husserl proposed that objects exist independently of the perceiving subject, but that our understanding of them is mediated by consciousness, whereby <em>experiences</em> shape the perception of those objects.</p><p>Perhaps the greatest mathematician of the 20th century, Alexander Grothendieck, revolutionised algebraic geometry using <em>unconventional</em> conventions, naming objects in informative yet <em>evocative</em> ways which reflected sometimes deeply personal perspectives.</p><blockquote><p>Grothendieck had a taste and a talent for naming things, which he used as a major intellectual strategy ... He had a special talent for naming things before possessing and conquering them, and many of his terminological choices were remarkable. <a href="https://www.ams.org/notices/201504/rnoti-p373.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">A Country Known Only by Name</a></p></blockquote><p>The idea that <em>possession</em> of (or <em>control</em> over) a thing could be somehow conferred by the very act of naming is an ancient concept that exists across a number of varied cultures and mythologies. In a folk tale that is <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-35358487" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">four thousand years old</a>, the life of the queen&#39;s firstborn is saved when she learns the name of her antagonist, <em>Rumpelstiltskin</em>.</p><blockquote><p>Without names there is nothing. If the mountain has no name, I did not climb it. I have not seen the painting without a name, not read the book without a title. Only the gymnastic skills with a name can be performed: cartwheel, straddle jump, cross split, Suzuki. That radical dependency on names is expressed in the Rumpelstiltskin fairy tale ... If she cannot guess the name, the girl, in the meantime queen, will lose her baby. <a href="https://www.sjaakvandergeest.socsci.uva.nl/pdf/anthropology_and_literature/rumpelskin.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Rumpelstiltskin: the magic of the right word</a></p></blockquote><p>In every aspect, names carry <em>identity</em>: the relation each thing bears only to itself. For humans, our identity is the set of characteristics that define us as <em>individual</em>. In programming, it is the fundamental property that identifies something <em>uniquely</em>. To name something is to have participated in a process of discovery, to have <em>identified</em> that by which it is <em>distinct</em> from other things.</p><blockquote><p>What is a thing but a name? To really name something you have to know what it is, and that is harder than you think.<br><br><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25427236" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ask HN: Why is naming things in programming so hard?</a></p></blockquote><p>The power of words, the nature of identity and the essence of being; these themes have resonated across antiquity, folklore, mysticism, science, philosophy, and software.</p><blockquote><p>Metaphorical thinking is pervasive in software development ... Software designs tend to be very abstract and hard to grasp. Developers and users alike need tangible ways to understand the system and share a view of the system as a whole. <a href="https://www.dddcommunity.org/book/evans_2003/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software</a></p></blockquote><p>Naming things <em>is</em> hard without a shared understanding or common vocabulary. Context is material when drawing the boundaries between identities, and context is defined by collective discovery. When we fail to seek a <em>ubiquitous language</em> through the process of collaborative identification, contexts are unknown or unclear, characteristics remain indistinct, and names elude us.</p><p>And when that happens, what we get, <em>metaphorically</em> speaking, is not a rose, but a <a href="http://www.laputan.org/mud/mud.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">big ball of mud</a>.</p>',21)]))}const u=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
